-- SLEEPSORT — the acceptance-test yardstick for any §11.7
-- effect-runner flip (see README.txt, finding 1).
--
-- The shape (v3): the input is a list of length l; a worker unwinds
-- it into l (N, n) tuples while counting, and hands the count to the
-- countdown gate. Each grab of N consumes one number and one counter
-- token, immediately re-arms the countdown ((Counter, r - 1)), then
-- sleeps proportional to n and says. The countdown's LAST token is
-- the exception: the (Counter, 1) holder re-arms nobody — instead it
-- emits the terminal (Bytes, Last) gate AFTER its own sleep and say
-- (a bytesBind completion is the only tuple that can land after a
-- delay — plain writes commit in-transaction), and (Bytes, Last)
-- closes the program. Exit therefore cannot precede the last
-- claimer's work.
--
-- What SHOULD happen (classic sleepsort): the l grabs sleep
-- concurrently, shortest first — output 1 1 3 4 5. That needs l
-- CONCURRENT instances of N, each delaying in its own thread; the
-- design above is written so that l instances (not l declarations)
-- are the only thing it asks of the runtime.
--
-- What DOES happen today (deterministic): one declaration = one
-- re-arming thread, and its [sleep n * 10; say n] bundles drain FIFO
-- through the single global effect runner, one fully live at a time
-- — so the sleeps contribute nothing and the output is grab order:
--
--   5 1 4 1 3   (outSTM prepends, so grabs walk the list backwards)
--
-- exit 0, cleanly, via the (Bytes, Last) gate. Sleep is honored
-- (each bundle really delays the runner) but orderless. If a §11.7
-- flip makes THIS file print 1 1 3 4 5, sleep has become actually
-- useful.
--
--   timeout 30 stack exec lindana -- sleepsort-not.lind

{
  (Sort, 0, [3, 1, 4, 1, 5])
}

-- worker: unwind the list into (N, n) tuples, counting as we go
(Sort, l, [h | t]) : [(N, h); (Sort, l + 1, t)]
(Sort, l, [])      : (Counter, l)

-- the sleeper: consume one number + one countdown token, re-arm the
-- countdown immediately, then delay and report — except the LAST
-- token holder, who closes the program after its own work
(N, n), (Counter, r) :
  if r == 1 then
    [sleep n * 10; say "%i" n; bytesBind Last [1]]
  else
    [(Counter, r - 1); sleep n * 10; say "%i" n]

(Bytes, Last) : exit 0
